      "White adds a second centre pawn and gains queenside space. The c4-pawn supports a later d4-d5 push, which is exactly the space-gaining advance you will often provoke with ...e5 in this opening.",
      "You prepare to fianchetto the bishop to g7, where it will press on d4 and the long a1-h8 diagonal. You are not contesting the centre with pawns yet, so White may take it. That is the bargain of the King's Indian.",
      "White develops the king's knight before the queen's knight and keeps e2-e4 in reserve. This move order often signals g3 and Bg2: a quieter system in which White aims for a solid king and pressure along the long diagonal rather than an early pawn storm.",
      "The bishop takes the long diagonal as usual. Your set-up hardly changes against White's systems: ...Bg7, ...O-O and ...d6 come first, and you choose between ...e5 and ...c5 once White shows a plan.",
      "White prepares Bg2. The fianchetto gives White a very safe king and a bishop that will look straight at your queenside, especially b7, once the long diagonal opens. It is one of the most respected answers to the King's Indian.",
      "Castling first is flexible: you have not committed the d-pawn or a knight yet. Your king is safe and you are ready to meet White's development with ...d6 and a central break.",
      "The bishop settles on g2. Because White's king will hide behind it, a direct kingside attack with ...f5 and ...g5, as in the Mar del Plata, is rarely realistic here. Your play will come in the centre instead.",
      "Control e5 and prepare ...e5. The c8-bishop's diagonal opens too. You keep the classical King's Indian structure, and the fight will be about the central squares e5, d4 and e4.",
